<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Newcastle West</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> West is a market town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-limerick">County Limerick</a>, Ireland. It grew around a castle of the FitzGerald earls of Desmond, who made it one of their main seats in western Munster. The restored Desmond Hall, a 15th-century banqueting hall within the castle complex, survives in the town centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/newcastle-west.jpg" alt="Newcastle West" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harold Strong · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newcastle_West,_The_Square.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The town, An Caisleán Nua Thiar in Irish, stands on the River Arra, a tributary of the Deel, in the middle of a broad bowl-shaped valley of West Limerick once known as the valley of the Wild Boar, apparently because the animal was plentiful when the area was thickly wooded; a boar appears on the town's crest. Its coat of arms carries the motto As Dúchas Dóchas, which may be translated as Our Hope springs from our Traditions. It lies on the N21 road from Limerick to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tralee">Tralee</a>, between Rathkeale and Abbeyfeale.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The FitzGerald family arrived some time after 1194 and had completed their castle in stone by 1298. The town was sacked in 1302 and destroyed in 1315. Two earls of Desmond died here: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerald-fitzgerald-3rd-earl-of-desmond">Gearóid Iarla</a> in 1399 and James, the 8th earl, in 1462. Three battles were fought nearby in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>. In the late 16th century the landlord, Sir William Courtenay, held 10,500 acres here; a Catholic, he was denounced in the House of Commons as a papist recusant in 1624.</p><p>The town held markets three days a week and fairs through the year, and its Thursday hiring fairs for farm workers drew large crowds. During the famine many hundreds of people who died in the workhouse were buried in unmarked graves behind what is now St. Ita's Hospital, and emigrants from across West Limerick took their leave at the Bridge of Tears before setting out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> and the voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. The Church of the Immaculate Conception was built in 1828 on a site given by the Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>. The railway reached the town on 1 January 1857 and closed in 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Newcastle West is the county town and, apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> city, the largest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-limerick">County Limerick</a>, with 7,209 people in 2022. The castle demesne holds about 99 acres of parkland, and the Desmond hall is open for guided tours from May to September. The old railway line is now the Great Southern Trail for walkers and cyclists. A monument unveiled by President Seán T. O'Kelly in 1955 commemorates 17 volunteers killed in the War of Independence and the Civil War.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Newcastle West” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Desmond earls ruled much of Munster from castles such as Askeaton and <b>Newcastle West</b>, adopting Irish language, law and patronage of poets.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-desmond">Earldom of Desmond</a></li></ul></section>
